            <ArticleTitle>Delivery of insulin to the buccal mucosa utilizing the RapidMist system.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1047-55</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1517/17425247.5.9.1047</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">The burgeoning number of people with diabetes mellitus is a global problem. Simple but effective treatment with minimal side effects will be required to reduce the risk for macro- and micro-vascular complications. A big part of this goal can be achieved by the ready administration of insulin with or without other medications.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The RapidMist drug delivery system places human recombinant insulin in a liquid formulation so as to be delivered to the buccal mucosa with an asthma-like device. As with nitroglycerin, the insulin PK-PD is very fast, thus affording flexibility.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Serial data show clinical efficacy in Type 1 and Type 2 diabetes with at least non-inferiority to regular insulin.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
